1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,258
Meet DataRove.

2
00:00:01,658 --> 00:00:03,374
It's one app for all your databases.

3
00:00:03,374 --> 00:00:07,567
Your backups actually get checked, and you can reach everything from your phone, safely.

4
00:00:10,491 --> 00:00:14,485
MySQL, Postgres, SQLite, SQL Server, MongoDB, Redis.

5
00:00:14,485 --> 00:00:16,559
They all live side by side.

6
00:00:16,959 --> 00:00:20,869
The overview shows your connections, what's scheduled, and how the last backups went.

7
00:00:21,269 --> 00:00:24,787
Passwords are encrypted with a key that never leaves this computer.

8
00:00:24,787 --> 00:00:26,520
SSH tunnels and TLS are built in.

9
00:00:32,082 --> 00:00:33,640
Open a table and scroll through it.

10
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:35,822
Sort the tree by size, so the big ones stand out.

11
00:00:37,932 --> 00:00:39,701
Filter, then just edit the cells.

12
00:00:49,551 --> 00:00:51,977
Before anything's saved, you see the exact SQL.

13
00:00:51,977 --> 00:00:53,991
It all goes through in one transaction.

14
00:00:55,257 --> 00:00:55,907
Prefer a form?

15
00:00:55,907 --> 00:00:57,858
Every column gets the right kind of input.

16
00:00:59,571 --> 00:01:02,569
For a foreign key, pick the related row instead of typing an ID.

17
00:01:06,328 --> 00:01:08,000
The SQL editor knows your schema.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,584
It completes tables, columns and joins as you type.

19
00:01:16,304 --> 00:01:16,675
Run it.

20
00:01:16,675 --> 00:01:20,285
Turn the result into a chart, or read the query plan in plain words.

21
00:01:22,259 --> 00:01:26,953
Everything you run is kept in the execution history, by connection and by table, so you can find it again.

22
00:01:31,795 --> 00:01:33,968
See the whole database as an ER diagram.

23
00:01:34,405 --> 00:01:37,488
Compare two databases, and get the script that brings them in line.

24
00:01:42,751 --> 00:01:47,058
Change a table's structure in a designer: columns, types, defaults and indexes.

25
00:01:47,458 --> 00:01:49,717
You review the generated SQL before it runs.

26
00:01:54,778 --> 00:01:58,905
Export or import millions of rows as CSV, JSON or SQL.

27
00:01:59,696 --> 00:02:03,938
It runs in the background with live progress, and picks up where it left off if the connection drops.

28
00:02:10,303 --> 00:02:11,689
Schedule backups to files.

29
00:02:11,689 --> 00:02:15,902
Compress them, encrypt them, and use the native tools or the built-in exporter.

30
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:19,398
Every new file is read back and checked, right after it's written.

31
00:02:22,742 --> 00:02:26,016
Or sync one database into another, even across database types.

32
00:02:26,016 --> 00:02:30,029
Each table lands completely, or not at all, and the row counts are verified.

33
00:02:30,429 --> 00:02:33,285
If something fails, retry just the part that failed.

34
00:02:33,285 --> 00:02:36,140
And if pg_dump is missing, it installs in one click.

35
00:02:37,403 --> 00:02:38,821
Restoring is just as easy.

36
00:02:38,821 --> 00:02:42,313
Pick a backup file, choose any connection, and restore it there.

37
00:02:42,713 --> 00:02:45,832
Incremental sync copies only the rows that changed since the last run.

38
00:02:50,765 --> 00:02:55,957
On production, DataRove saves the affected rows before an UPDATE, a DELETE or a DROP touches them.

39
00:02:59,124 --> 00:02:59,915
Made a mistake?

40
00:02:59,915 --> 00:03:01,023
Undo it in one click.

41
00:03:05,907 --> 00:03:09,622
The server monitor shows sessions, queries per second and slow queries.

42
00:03:10,022 --> 00:03:15,605
When a backup fails or a server goes down, you get a message in Feishu, DingTalk, WeCom, Slack or Discord.

43
00:03:20,759 --> 00:03:25,781
Let Claude Code, Cursor or Codex read your schema and run read-only queries, over MCP.

44
00:03:26,187 --> 00:03:27,776
You choose which connections they see.

45
00:03:27,776 --> 00:03:30,954
They never get a password, and every query they run shows up in the history.

46
00:03:34,008 --> 00:03:35,475
Your phone gets the same app.

47
00:03:35,475 --> 00:03:36,639
Scan a QR code to pair.

48
00:03:36,639 --> 00:03:37,550
No account needed.

49
00:03:39,526 --> 00:03:42,197
On the same Wi-Fi, it connects straight to your computer.

50
00:03:42,197 --> 00:03:45,759
When you're out, it goes through a relay that only ever sees encrypted data.

51
00:03:46,159 --> 00:03:50,785
It's end-to-end encrypted, passwords stay on your computer, and each phone gets its own permissions.

52
00:03:52,085 --> 00:03:52,983
No computer nearby?

53
00:03:52,983 --> 00:03:55,630
The Android app can connect to your databases by itself.

54
00:03:56,037 --> 00:03:58,016
Passwords and backups stay on the phone.

55
00:03:58,016 --> 00:04:00,488
Every account includes one computer and one phone.

56
00:04:03,410 --> 00:04:04,756
There's a key browser for Redis.

57
00:04:08,837 --> 00:04:12,205
And a command palette that finds any table, connection or action.

58
00:04:12,605 --> 00:04:14,780
Pick from ten themes and fifteen languages.

59
00:04:16,243 --> 00:04:19,978
And a background service keeps your scheduled backups running, even with the window closed.

60
00:04:21,278 --> 00:04:21,778
DataRove.

61
00:04:21,778 --> 00:04:24,944
For macOS, Windows and Linux, with an app for your phone.

62
00:04:25,344 --> 00:04:26,000
Get it on GitHub.
